module, this cylinder that contained the main rocket

250
00:15:01,381 --> 00:15:04,800
engine that they would get into and out of lunar orbit.

251
00:15:04,801 --> 00:15:07,320
The service module engine was responsible for putting them

252
00:15:07,321 --> 00:15:10,240
into orbit around the moon, and more importantly,

253
00:15:10,241 --> 00:15:15,240
taking them out of that orbit and sending them back home.

254
00:15:15,241 --> 00:15:18,480
Three days after launch, Collins fired the engine,

255
00:15:18,481 --> 00:15:22,420
and the 21,000 pounds of thrust slowed the spacecraft, allowing

256
00:15:22,421 --> 00:15:26,360
it to fall into a lunar orbit.

257
00:15:26,361 --> 00:15:29,040
And the engine was designed beautifully

258
00:15:29,041 --> 00:15:32,620
with as much simplicity as possible.

259
00:15:32,621 --> 00:15:36,760
It was an engine that used the fuel that was called hypergolic.

260
00:15:36,761 --> 00:15:40,100
Hypergolic fuel is fuel that will ignite on contact,

261
00:15:40,101 --> 00:15:44,100
so you don't need an ignition system.

262
00:15:44,101 --> 00:15:46,900
Apollo 11 was in lunar orbit.

263
00:15:46,901 --> 00:15:49,640
Their voyage had been long, and they were ready to get on

264
00:15:49,641 --> 00:15:51,800
with the next phase of the mission,

265
00:15:51,801 --> 00:15:55,880
to attempt a landing on the moon.

266
00:15:55,881 --> 00:15:58,740
But navigating the command module and lunar module

267
00:15:58,741 --> 00:16:02,980
involved challenges never before faced by any pilot.

268
00:16:02,981 --> 00:16:06,120
In an age when computers could fill an entire room,

269
00:16:06,121 --> 00:16:10,960
something compact and very smart would be needed to steer these spacecraft.

270
00:16:10,961 --> 00:16:14,160
The guidance and navigation system was amazing.

271
00:16:14,161 --> 00:16:19,160
And today, when I look back at it 35 years ago, it was even more amazing.

272
00:16:19,161 --> 00:16:24,080
It had all of 60,000 words in it.

273
00:16:24,081 --> 00:16:28,880
Today, if you don't have gigabytes of memory, you don't seem to be able to get along.

274
00:16:28,881 --> 00:16:36,080
The computer that landed in the landing module was built specifically one time for that mission.

275
00:16:36,081 --> 00:16:39,380
At 70 pounds and about one square foot in size,

276
00:16:39,480 --> 00:16:43,380
it was considered compact. The lunar module computer was a

277
00:16:43,480 --> 00:16:45,720
marvel of engineering for its time.

278
00:16:45,820 --> 00:16:48,820
An identical twin would steer the command module.

279
00:16:48,920 --> 00:16:54,440
The little handheld calculator that you could go in and buy at

280
00:16:54,540 --> 00:16:59,940
Walmart would be as powerful as the guidance computer we had on

281
00:17:00,040 --> 00:17:04,440
the landing vehicle. But as Armstrong and Aldrin

282
00:17:04,441 --> 00:17:07,680
were preparing the lunar module to descend, Mission Control was

283
00:17:07,681 --> 00:17:11,240
worried about an unanticipated event that could greatly impact

284
00:17:11,241 --> 00:17:15,680
Apollo 11. The Apollo command module could

285
00:17:15,681 --> 00:17:19,240
sustain a quarter-inch hold and still maintain survivable cabin

286
00:17:19,241 --> 00:17:22,240
pressure for 15 minutes, providing the astronauts enough

287
00:17:22,241 --> 00:17:27,840
time to get into their spacesuits. July 20th.

288
00:17:27,841 --> 00:17:31,840
Apollo 11 had been orbiting the moon for just under a day.

289
00:17:31,841 --> 00:17:35,640
As Armstrong and Aldrin powered up the LM and began hours of

290
00:17:35,641 --> 00:17:39,080
checks and double checks, they received worrisome news from the

291
00:17:39,081 --> 00:17:41,640
ground. Mission control was fretting over

292
00:17:41,641 --> 00:17:45,880
an intruder into their mission. The Soviets had sent an unmanned

293
00:17:45,881 --> 00:17:49,080
probe into lunar orbit, coinciding with the arrival of

294
00:17:49,081 --> 00:17:52,200
Apollo 11. Attempts had been made to contact

295
00:17:52,201 --> 00:17:55,880
the Russians, but nobody was as yet quite clear on what the rogue

296
00:17:55,881 --> 00:17:59,600
craft was for, or if its orbit might present a danger to the

297
00:17:59,601 --> 00:18:02,440
with the Apollo 11 crew.

298
00:18:02,441 --> 00:18:04,240
The Soviets were actually trying to pull off

299
00:18:04,241 --> 00:18:07,600
a sample return mission, a robotic sample return mission.

300
00:18:07,601 --> 00:18:11,940
It was called Luna 15, and it was supposed to land on the moon,

301
00:18:11,941 --> 00:18:14,040
collect a sample, and bring it back to Earth.

302
00:18:14,041 --> 00:18:19,620
It certainly didn't hurt that it would have upstaged Apollo 11,

303
00:18:19,621 --> 00:18:20,620
but it didn't.

304
00:18:20,621 --> 00:18:24,300
It crashed on the moon.

305
00:18:24,301 --> 00:18:26,100
With the Soviet threat eliminated,

306
00:18:26,101 --> 00:18:29,840
awaited the go from Houston for powered descent.

307
00:18:29,841 --> 00:18:32,740
They would be the first either to reach the surface, abort a

308
00:18:32,741 --> 00:18:37,940
landing, or die trying. The lunar module was the first

309
00:18:37,941 --> 00:18:41,140
true spacecraft ever built, in that it would never operate in

310
00:18:41,141 --> 00:18:43,860
an atmosphere. Our design, of course, at that

311
00:18:43,861 --> 00:18:47,760
time was mainly driven by aeronautical engineers.

312
00:18:47,761 --> 00:18:50,760
The lunar module, of course, was not going to be constrained

313
00:18:50,761 --> 00:18:53,360
with aerodynamics. It was going to operate in the

314
00:18:53,361 --> 00:18:55,860
vacuum of space. But to land on the

315
00:18:55,861 --> 00:18:59,700
on and depart from the moon, the LEM had to be lightened.

316
00:18:59,800 --> 00:19:02,500
The earlier lunar modules had been too heavy.

317
00:19:02,600 --> 00:19:05,400
So they started throwing away all of the extra stuff.

318
00:19:05,500 --> 00:19:07,900
They took out the seats because the astronauts

319
00:19:08,000 --> 00:19:11,500
would only fly in 1,6 gravity or in weightlessness,

320
00:19:11,600 --> 00:19:14,120
and so they didn't need seats.

321
00:19:14,220 --> 00:19:15,720
The weight saving program produced

322
00:19:15,820 --> 00:19:18,420
a lunar module that was capable, but structurally

323
00:19:18,520 --> 00:19:20,620
very lightly built.

324
00:19:20,720 --> 00:19:23,420
Instead of a metal skin, much of the lower stage

325
00:19:23,421 --> 00:19:26,320
was simply wrapped in mylar foil.

326
00:19:26,420 --> 00:19:29,020
Some of the astronauts took to calling it the tissue paper

327
00:19:29,120 --> 00:19:29,920
spacecraft.

328
00:19:30,020 --> 00:19:32,060
Even the parts where there were metal,

329
00:19:32,160 --> 00:19:35,860
like the skin of the crew cabin, was just a fraction

330
00:19:35,960 --> 00:19:37,260
of an inch thick.

331
00:19:37,360 --> 00:19:42,380
So this was, it was like a very taut aluminum balloon.

332
00:19:42,480 --> 00:19:44,980
The overall design of the lunar module pushed Grumman

333
00:19:45,080 --> 00:19:47,180
engineers to their limits.

334
00:19:47,280 --> 00:19:50,540
A particularly difficult task was designing the descent stage

335
00:19:50,541 --> 00:19:52,540
that would land them on the moon.

336
00:19:52,541 --> 00:19:55,340
It had to have variable thrust to allow them to hover

337
00:19:55,341 --> 00:19:57,040
and search for a landing site.

338
00:19:57,041 --> 00:19:59,880
So they developed this engine that could be throttled

339
00:19:59,881 --> 00:20:00,880
like a car.

340
00:20:00,881 --> 00:20:03,080
And if you look at the landing profile,

341
00:20:03,081 --> 00:20:06,080
you see that it starts out at 100%,

342
00:20:06,081 --> 00:20:10,000
and then it throttles down partway through.

343
00:20:10,001 --> 00:20:12,300
Then there was the ascent engine.

344
00:20:12,301 --> 00:20:15,340
Its only task was to return the two men to orbit.

345
00:20:15,341 --> 00:20:18,300
But if it failed, the men would be doomed

346
00:20:18,301 --> 00:20:21,800
doomed to spend the rest of their short lives on the moon.

347
00:20:21,900 --> 00:20:26,200
The reliability of that one had to be perfect, you know,

348
00:20:26,300 --> 00:20:30,440
because you're not leaving if that thing doesn't work.

349
00:20:30,540 --> 00:20:37,360
So the idea was in rocketry is simple means high reliability.

350
00:20:37,460 --> 00:20:40,160
The only moving parts were two valves.

351
00:20:40,260 --> 00:20:44,160
If they were opened, the ascent module would fly.

352
00:20:44,161 --> 00:20:48,060
It was 4 p.m. on July 20, Eastern Daylight Time.

353
00:20:48,160 --> 00:20:52,760
Over 102 hours into the mission, the Eagle was ready for a landing

354
00:20:52,860 --> 00:20:55,600
on the moon. Michael Collins, alone in the

355
00:20:55,700 --> 00:21:00,000
command module, slowly circled the moon in a different orbit.

356
00:21:00,100 --> 00:21:03,020
He was standing by either to attempt a rescue if the landing

357
00:21:03,120 --> 00:21:06,820
was aborted or cheer if it succeeded.

358
00:21:06,920 --> 00:21:10,160
Both spacecraft were now behind the moon and out of communication

359
00:21:10,260 --> 00:21:13,120
with Earth. On the ground in Houston,

360
00:21:13,121 --> 00:21:15,820
Gene Kranz was the flight director for the landing.

361
00:21:15,920 --> 00:21:18,820
As he listened to static, waiting for the eagle to emerge

362
00:21:18,920 --> 00:21:21,160
from behind the moon, he used this moment

363
00:21:21,260 --> 00:21:22,860
for one last pep talk.

364
00:21:22,960 --> 00:21:25,000
So I called my controllers onto this loop

365
00:21:25,100 --> 00:21:27,160
for a very personal conversation,

366
00:21:27,260 --> 00:21:31,180
because this was very emotional for me, emotional moment.

367
00:21:31,280 --> 00:21:37,680
And I really wanted to tell my team how much I believed in them

368
00:21:37,780 --> 00:21:40,620
and what I thought of them and what I felt they were capable

369
00:21:40,621 --> 00:21:50,620
And that even though this is something that had never been done before, I believe that, A, we were the right team at the right place.

370
00:21:50,621 --> 00:21:53,620
And this day, we would make it happen.

371
00:21:53,621 --> 00:21:59,620
And from that moment forward, no controller would enter or leave the room until one of three things had happened.

372
00:21:59,621 --> 00:22:05,620
We would either land on the moon, we would crash attempting to land, or we would abort.

373
00:22:05,621 --> 00:22:09,620
And the final two outcomes were not good.

374
00:22:09,621 --> 00:22:12,820
The lunar module was now back in communication,

375
00:22:12,920 --> 00:22:15,160
but the link with the ground was spotty.

376
00:22:15,260 --> 00:22:16,820
Gene Kranz was concerned.

377
00:22:16,920 --> 00:22:21,360
So it's purely my decision as to how much information

378
00:22:21,460 --> 00:22:22,560
is enough.

379
00:22:22,660 --> 00:22:25,160
Well, the kind of information you're looking for is telemetry

380
00:22:25,260 --> 00:22:27,780
information and voice information.

381
00:22:27,880 --> 00:22:30,480
So this is now going through my mind,

382
00:22:30,580 --> 00:22:32,480
do I have enough to continue?

383
00:22:32,580 --> 00:22:33,980
And the answer was yes.

384
00:22:34,080 --> 00:22:34,920
OK, retro.

385
00:22:35,020 --> 00:22:35,720
Go.

386
00:22:35,820 --> 00:22:36,520
Fido.

387
00:22:36,521 --> 00:22:37,221
Go.

388
00:22:37,222 --> 00:22:37,922
Guide.

389
00:22:37,923 --> 00:22:38,623
Go.

390
00:22:38,624 --> 00:22:39,324
Control.

391
00:22:39,325 --> 00:22:40,025
Go.

392
00:22:40,026 --> 00:22:40,726
GINC.

393
00:22:40,727 --> 00:22:41,427
Go.

394
00:22:41,428 --> 00:22:42,128
E-COM.

395
00:22:42,129 --> 00:22:42,829
Go.

396
00:22:42,830 --> 00:22:43,530
Surgeon.

397
00:22:43,531 --> 00:22:44,231
Go.

398
00:22:44,232 --> 00:22:44,932
CAPCOM, we're go to continue PDI.

399
00:22:44,933 --> 00:22:49,420
So I gave the crew the go to the go for power descent.

400
00:22:49,421 --> 00:22:51,320
You go to continue power descent.

401
00:22:51,321 --> 00:22:53,520
You're a go to continue power descent.

402
00:22:53,521 --> 00:22:55,820
The crew acknowledged their go, and we immediately

403
00:22:55,821 --> 00:22:57,820
lost communications again.

404
00:22:57,821 --> 00:23:00,020
Despite the problems, the landing engine

405
00:23:00,021 --> 00:23:02,220
fired right on time, and the Eagle

406
00:23:02,221 --> 00:23:04,520
began its long descent towards the moon.

407
00:23:04,521 --> 00:23:08,620
Of course, we were going engine first, face down,

408
00:23:08,621 --> 00:23:13,260
so that we could see the landmarks moving from the bottom

409
00:23:13,261 --> 00:23:15,660
of the window up to the top of the window,

410
00:23:15,661 --> 00:23:19,980
and we could make time and landmark checks.

411
00:23:19,981 --> 00:23:24,180
We had very crude instrumentation and capability

412
00:23:24,181 --> 00:23:25,080
measuring at that time.

413
00:23:25,081 --> 00:23:27,820
But it was good enough to get the crew started down

414
00:23:27,821 --> 00:23:29,020
towards the surface of the moon.

415
00:23:29,021 --> 00:23:31,360
But as they got closer to the surface,

416
00:23:31,361 --> 00:23:33,720
we had to bring in the landing radar

417
00:23:33,721 --> 00:23:35,760
so it could update the crew's knowledge

418
00:23:35,761 --> 00:23:38,380
of the actual altitude.

419
00:23:38,381 --> 00:23:41,720
As they lost altitude, Armstrong's intense concentration

420
00:23:41,721 --> 00:23:44,140
was fixed on the surface outside.

421
00:23:44,141 --> 00:23:46,620
Aldrin was scanning the LEM instrumentation,

422
00:23:46,621 --> 00:23:48,680
relaying information to Houston.

423
00:23:48,681 --> 00:23:51,380
Then, with no warning, their troubles began.

424
00:23:51,381 --> 00:23:53,380
That's a program alarm.

425
00:23:53,381 --> 00:23:55,380
It's a 12-02.

426
00:23:55,381 --> 00:23:56,620
12-02.

427
00:23:56,621 --> 00:24:00,180
At 34,000 feet, they had serious problems.

428
00:24:00,181 --> 00:24:02,740
Their computer was issuing an overload alarm,

429
00:24:02,741 --> 00:24:05,080
and they were only six minutes from the ground.

430
00:24:05,081 --> 00:24:07,880
And the computer is throwing out all these alarms

431
00:24:07,881 --> 00:24:10,860
and saying, you know, I've got too much to do,

432
00:24:10,861 --> 00:24:12,500
and if you don't lighten my workload,

433
00:24:12,501 --> 00:24:13,740
I'm just going to give up.

434
00:24:13,741 --> 00:24:16,760
When they did come on, we couldn't look it up in the book

435
00:24:16,761 --> 00:24:18,420
to see what the problem was, because we

436
00:24:18,421 --> 00:24:21,880
were watching where we were going, calling out altitude,

437
00:24:21,881 --> 00:24:22,780
altitude rate.

438
00:24:22,781 --> 00:24:26,300
You're not exactly sure which direction, which path to take.

439
00:24:26,301 --> 00:24:28,360
Do we keep working for the landing radar thing,

440
00:24:28,361 --> 00:24:30,320
or do we start addressing this question of the computer

441
00:24:30,321 --> 00:24:31,260
program alarm?

442
00:24:31,261 --> 00:24:35,180
It was enough to interrupt our train of thought

443
00:24:35,181 --> 00:24:37,640
and get us wondering, when's the next one going

444
00:24:37,641 --> 00:24:41,880
Right in the middle of landing, it was almost as dangerous

445
00:24:41,980 --> 00:24:43,880
to try and abort with a bad computer

446
00:24:43,980 --> 00:24:46,380
as it was to carry on with the landing.

447
00:24:46,480 --> 00:24:48,880
But the moon continued to grow in their windows,

448
00:24:48,980 --> 00:24:50,280
and the alarms continued.

449
00:24:50,380 --> 00:24:51,280
Go for landing.

450
00:24:51,380 --> 00:24:52,280
3,000 feet.

451
00:24:52,380 --> 00:24:53,680
Got alarm.

452
00:24:53,780 --> 00:24:54,700
1201.

453
00:24:54,800 --> 00:24:55,600
1201.

454
00:24:55,700 --> 00:24:56,600
Roger.

455
00:24:56,700 --> 00:24:58,000
1201 alarm.

456
00:24:58,100 --> 00:25:01,600
The 1201 alarm was more urgent than the 1202.

457
00:25:01,700 --> 00:25:04,900
This meant that the computer might quit completely.

458
00:25:05,000 --> 00:25:07,620
As Aldrin continued to relay their numbers to our

459
00:25:07,640 --> 00:25:10,140
They had to find a place to set down.

460
00:25:10,141 --> 00:25:13,640
They were 2,000 feet and two minutes from the rocks below.

461
00:25:13,641 --> 00:25:16,640
As I was looking at my trajectory plot,

462
00:25:16,641 --> 00:25:19,640
Neil leveled off at about 400 feet

463
00:25:19,641 --> 00:25:23,640
and was whizzing across the surface, and it wasn't the...

464
00:25:23,641 --> 00:25:28,640
It was far from what we trained for and seen in the simulation.

465
00:25:28,641 --> 00:25:32,640
Neil is looking out the window, and he sees this enormous crater.

466
00:25:32,641 --> 00:25:35,640
This is a crater the size of a football field

467
00:25:35,641 --> 00:25:39,940
with boulders around it that are as big as small cars, you know?

468
00:25:39,941 --> 00:25:41,640
We continue on down the surface.

469
00:25:41,641 --> 00:25:43,980
We should have been on the surface by about 10 minutes.

470
00:25:43,981 --> 00:25:47,680
And now, as a result of this trajectory problem we had,

471
00:25:47,681 --> 00:25:51,280
we're further downrange from our landing site.

472
00:25:51,281 --> 00:25:53,100
As they neared the lunar surface,

473
00:25:53,101 --> 00:25:54,900
Armstrong prepared to wrest control

474
00:25:54,901 --> 00:25:56,300
from the errant computer.

475
00:25:56,301 --> 00:25:57,900
Now we get to about 500 feet,

476
00:25:57,901 --> 00:26:00,900
and we take over manual control.

477
00:26:00,901 --> 00:26:04,300
And at this time, I think Neil could really see

478
00:26:04,301 --> 00:26:06,940
that it was going where he didn't want it to go.

479
00:26:07,040 --> 00:26:09,880
They were nearing the point called Dead Man's Curve.

480
00:26:09,980 --> 00:26:13,280
If they dipped below this, it would be too late to abort.

481
00:26:13,380 --> 00:26:15,540
If you ever go below the Dead Man's Curve,

482
00:26:15,640 --> 00:26:19,820
you just don't have enough power to go back up

483
00:26:19,920 --> 00:26:22,920
and do an abort before you had crashed.

484
00:26:23,020 --> 00:26:25,420
Fuel was now getting critically low.

485
00:26:25,520 --> 00:26:28,460
Houston signaled 60 seconds until an abort.

486
00:26:28,560 --> 00:26:31,560
60 seconds. 60 seconds.

487
00:26:31,561 --> 00:26:34,900
And we did hear the call of 60 seconds,

488
00:26:35,000 --> 00:26:36,960
and a low-level light came on.

489
00:26:37,060 --> 00:26:40,040
That, I'm sure, caused concern in the control center.

490
00:26:40,140 --> 00:26:43,340
They normally expected us to land with about two minutes

491
00:26:43,440 --> 00:26:44,440
of fuel left.

492
00:26:44,540 --> 00:26:48,080
And here we were down, still 100 feet above the surface

493
00:26:48,180 --> 00:26:49,880
at 60 seconds.

494
00:26:49,980 --> 00:26:53,020
But when I called one minute, they were starting down.

495
00:26:53,120 --> 00:26:55,320
And then I called 30 seconds.

496
00:26:55,420 --> 00:26:58,620
And we were really, I was really getting a little anxious

497
00:26:58,720 --> 00:26:59,580
at this point.

498
00:26:59,581 --> 00:27:03,320
At about that time, you know, you really start to suck air.

499
00:27:03,420 --> 00:27:07,320
And I'm seriously thinking now we have this land abort decision

500
00:27:07,420 --> 00:27:08,920
that the crew is faced with.

501
00:27:09,020 --> 00:27:12,720
Are they going to have sufficient fuel to land on the moon?

502
00:27:12,820 --> 00:27:14,520
Or are they going to have to abort very close

503
00:27:14,620 --> 00:27:16,840
to the lunar surface, which is extremely difficult

504
00:27:16,940 --> 00:27:18,440
and extremely risky?

505
00:27:18,540 --> 00:27:19,240
30 seconds.

506
00:27:19,241 --> 00:27:20,940
Forward, good.

507
00:27:21,040 --> 00:27:22,740
When it got down to 30 seconds,

508
00:27:22,840 --> 00:27:24,980
we were about 10 feet or less.

509
00:27:25,080 --> 00:27:28,880
And I could see that they were in the final phases.

510
00:27:28,881 --> 00:27:32,380
A little bit of drifting, and I could sneak a lookout,

511
00:27:32,480 --> 00:27:34,560
because at that point, I don't think Neil

512
00:27:34,660 --> 00:27:35,820
cared what the numbers were.

513
00:27:35,920 --> 00:27:37,360
He was looking at the outside.

514
00:27:37,460 --> 00:27:39,060
Contact light.

515
00:27:39,160 --> 00:27:41,500
We copy you down, Eagle.

516
00:27:41,600 --> 00:27:44,500
Brisbane Tranquility Base here.

517
00:27:44,600 --> 00:27:46,000
The Eagle has landed.

518
00:27:46,100 --> 00:27:47,200
Roger, Twink.

519
00:27:47,300 --> 00:27:49,300
Tranquility, we copy you on the ground.

520
00:27:49,400 --> 00:27:51,400
You got a bunch of guys about to turn blue.

521
00:27:51,500 --> 00:27:52,400
We're breathing again.

522
00:27:52,500 --> 00:27:53,400
Thanks a lot.

523
00:27:55,580 --> 00:27:58,880
Touchdown was at 4.18 PM Eastern Daylight.

524
00:27:58,881 --> 00:28:00,880
they were four miles off target.

525
00:28:00,881 --> 00:28:04,380
Just 17 seconds of fuel remained for the landing.

526
00:28:04,381 --> 00:28:06,880
It took us a couple seconds to really realize

527
00:28:06,881 --> 00:28:08,380
that we had made it.

528
00:28:08,381 --> 00:28:09,880
And then the people in the viewing room

529
00:28:09,881 --> 00:28:13,380
start cheering and clapping and stomping their feet.

530
00:28:13,381 --> 00:28:16,880
And I'm so tied up emotionally at this time

531
00:28:16,881 --> 00:28:19,880
that I literally cannot speak, and I've got to get my team

532
00:28:19,881 --> 00:28:20,880
back on track.

533
00:28:20,881 --> 00:28:25,880
And I wrap my arm on the console and break my pencil

534
00:28:25,881 --> 00:28:29,180
and finally get back on track and call my controllers

535
00:28:29,181 --> 00:28:31,420
to attention, say, OK, all you flight controllers,

536
00:28:31,421 --> 00:28:33,020
settle down.

537
00:28:33,021 --> 00:28:34,920
OK, let's get back on with it.

538
00:28:34,921 --> 00:28:38,420
Apollo 11 was on the moon, but beneath the two astronauts

539
00:28:38,421 --> 00:28:40,420
in the lunar module's descent stage,

540
00:28:40,421 --> 00:28:43,340
a malfunction was brewing that could end the mission

541
00:28:43,341 --> 00:28:45,340
in a catastrophic explosion.

542
00:28:45,341 --> 00:28:51,340
The power of one Saturn V was enough to place in Earth orbit

543
00:28:51,341 --> 00:28:55,880
all US manned spacecraft launched before the Apollo program.

544
00:28:55,881 --> 00:29:00,880
The Lunar Module Eagle had been on the moon for about an hour

545
00:29:00,881 --> 00:29:03,880
by the time Armstrong and Aldrin were completing their post-landing

546
00:29:03,881 --> 00:29:06,880
checks. During that time, disaster had

547
00:29:06,881 --> 00:29:10,880
been brewing just beneath their feet in the descent stage.

548
00:29:10,881 --> 00:29:16,880
The cold on the moon permeated the fuel line and caused a

549
00:29:16,881 --> 00:29:19,880
blockage in the fuel line, which was immediately reported back

550
00:29:19,881 --> 00:29:23,880
by telemetry to mission control in Houston.

551
00:29:23,881 --> 00:29:25,880
That gave us cause for alarm.

552
00:29:25,980 --> 00:29:28,380
The pipe that had supplied fuel to the descent engine

553
00:29:28,480 --> 00:29:31,380
was plugged, and pressure was building up rapidly.

554
00:29:31,480 --> 00:29:34,520
If it didn't clear on its own, Kranz would have had no choice

555
00:29:34,620 --> 00:29:37,520
but to order and abort and head back to orbit.

556
00:29:37,620 --> 00:29:40,840
There was, for the few seconds that this was going on,

557
00:29:40,940 --> 00:29:44,340
some thought given to aborting the exploration of the moon

558
00:29:44,440 --> 00:29:47,840
and to initiate the launch sequence right away.

559
00:29:47,940 --> 00:29:52,400
However, the heat that came out of the engine melted the ice

560
00:29:52,401 --> 00:29:55,300
that had formed in the line, and the problem went away.

561
00:29:55,400 --> 00:29:56,900
Disaster averted.

562
00:29:57,000 --> 00:30:00,000
Armstrong and Aldrin prepared to explore the moon.

563
00:30:00,100 --> 00:30:02,740
Six hours after landing, the two astronauts

564
00:30:02,840 --> 00:30:05,740
were ready to depart the lunar module.

565
00:30:05,840 --> 00:30:07,540
Now clad in their bulky moon suits,

566
00:30:07,640 --> 00:30:09,620
they slowly vented the air inside the lamp

567
00:30:09,720 --> 00:30:11,360
so they could open the hatch.

568
00:30:11,460 --> 00:30:14,460
When we're all ready and depressurized,

569
00:30:14,560 --> 00:30:18,100
we're looking at the gauge as we turn the valve,

570
00:30:18,101 --> 00:30:23,600
turned the valve, I think, on the handle to vent the pressure

571
00:30:23,601 --> 00:30:25,640
four or five pounds per square inch.

572
00:30:25,641 --> 00:30:27,860
And it went down to the point where

573
00:30:27,861 --> 00:30:30,660
it was almost zero fairly quickly.

574
00:30:30,661 --> 00:30:32,880
We thought, well, let's give it a try.

575
00:30:32,881 --> 00:30:34,780
Tried to pull the door open.

576
00:30:34,781 --> 00:30:36,160
It wouldn't come open.

577
00:30:36,161 --> 00:30:39,540
We thought, well, I wonder if we're going to get out or not.

578
00:30:39,541 --> 00:30:42,880
And it took an abnormal time for it

579
00:30:42,881 --> 00:30:46,240
to finally get to a point where we could pull on a fairly

580
00:30:46,241 --> 00:30:50,940
flimsy door, and you don't want to rupture that door and leave

581
00:30:50,941 --> 00:30:55,240
yourself in a vacuum for the entire rest of the mission.

582
00:30:55,241 --> 00:30:57,380
Aldrin tugged on the edge of the thin hatch,

583
00:30:57,381 --> 00:31:02,180
and it swung open at last.

584
00:31:02,181 --> 00:31:08,200
As Aldrin guided him, Armstrong carefully left the cabin.

585
00:31:08,201 --> 00:31:11,200
After a cautious climb down the spindly ladder,

586
00:31:11,201 --> 00:31:13,600
Armstrong was standing on the foot pad.

587
00:31:13,601 --> 00:31:15,840
It was time to make history.

588
00:31:15,841 --> 00:31:19,380
I'm at the foot of the ladder.

589
00:31:19,381 --> 00:31:20,780
I'm going to step off the land now.

590
00:31:30,380 --> 00:31:39,240
That's one small step for man, one giant leap for mankind.

591
00:31:39,241 --> 00:31:44,800
It was 10.56 PM Eastern Daylight Time.

592
00:31:44,801 --> 00:31:47,300
As Aldrin prepared to egress, Armstrong made his first

593
00:31:47,301 --> 00:31:49,800
observations of the lunar surface.

594
00:31:49,801 --> 00:31:51,800
The surface is fine and powdery.

595
00:31:51,801 --> 00:31:55,800
I can pick it up loosely with my toe.

596
00:31:55,801 --> 00:31:59,800
I only go in a small fraction of an inch, maybe an eighth of an

597
00:31:59,801 --> 00:32:02,800
inch, but I can see.

598
00:32:02,801 --> 00:32:04,800
It has a stark beauty all its own.

599
00:32:04,801 --> 00:32:08,800
It's like much of the high desert of the United States.

600
00:32:08,801 --> 00:32:12,800
It's different, but it's very pretty out here.

601
00:32:12,801 --> 00:32:15,700
Within the half hour, Aldrin was also out of the LEM,

602
00:32:15,701 --> 00:32:18,400
climbing down the ladder.

603
00:32:18,401 --> 00:32:19,940
There you got it.

604
00:32:19,941 --> 00:32:20,940
That's a good step.

605
00:32:20,941 --> 00:32:22,200
Yep.

606
00:32:22,201 --> 00:32:25,120
About a three-footer.

607
00:32:25,121 --> 00:32:28,540
As Aldrin got to the footpad, he turned to see the view

608
00:32:28,541 --> 00:32:30,360
and was awestruck.

609
00:32:30,361 --> 00:32:32,780
Magnificent desolation.

610
00:32:32,781 --> 00:32:37,360
The visibility was so clear, especially looking down sun.

611
00:32:37,361 --> 00:32:39,920
If you looked down at the surface,

612
00:32:39,921 --> 00:32:45,320
You could clearly see a glow around the shadow of your head.

613
00:32:45,321 --> 00:32:49,100
Even in shadows where the sun didn't reach,

614
00:32:49,101 --> 00:32:52,260
you could see into the shadows reasonably well.

615
00:32:52,261 --> 00:32:54,900
Looking out on the horizon, it was so clear,

616
00:32:54,901 --> 00:32:58,100
you could see the moon curve away.

617
00:32:58,101 --> 00:33:00,640
Estimating distance was kind of difficult,

618
00:33:00,641 --> 00:33:04,240
because you don't know exactly how far away it's going to curve.

619
00:33:04,241 --> 00:33:08,040
And the clarity is so distinct.

620
00:33:08,041 --> 00:33:10,880
the two explorers began to test their mobility

621
00:33:10,881 --> 00:33:12,480
and plant the American flag.

622
00:33:12,481 --> 00:33:15,480
A lot of people think because you have one-sixth gravity

623
00:33:15,481 --> 00:33:18,920
that you ought to be able to jump six times higher.

624
00:33:18,921 --> 00:33:20,020
Wrong.

625
00:33:20,021 --> 00:33:24,360
You can jump to the degree that you can accelerate your mass

626
00:33:24,361 --> 00:33:26,700
from a standing still movement.

627
00:33:26,701 --> 00:33:29,220
As soon as your feet leave the ground, you're done.

628
00:33:29,221 --> 00:33:31,040
You can't do anything more.

629
00:33:31,041 --> 00:33:34,200
So it's the rapidity of that motion.

630
00:33:34,201 --> 00:33:37,200
And is that hampered by the stiffness of the suit?

631
00:33:37,201 --> 00:33:39,300
Yes, it is.

632
00:33:39,301 --> 00:33:42,540
Despite the thick, bulky suits, the two men quickly set about

633
00:33:42,541 --> 00:33:44,240
their other chores.

634
00:33:44,241 --> 00:33:47,740
One of these was to set up a simple science station, far

635
00:33:47,741 --> 00:33:50,840
smaller than what would be used on later missions.

636
00:33:50,841 --> 00:33:54,440
They created something called ESEP, which stood for Early

637
00:33:54,441 --> 00:33:56,860
Apollo Scientific Experiments Package.

638
00:33:56,861 --> 00:34:01,160
And it consisted basically of a seismometer that would detect

639
00:34:01,161 --> 00:34:03,100
moon quakes.

640
00:34:03,101 --> 00:34:06,340
And it was powered by solar panels.

641
00:34:06,341 --> 00:34:13,340
And a reflector called a laser-ranging retro reflector.

642
00:34:13,341 --> 00:34:15,340
The ESEP would remain on the moon

643
00:34:15,341 --> 00:34:18,520
and send back data for five weeks.

644
00:34:18,521 --> 00:34:20,280
The laser reflector would allow astronomers

645
00:34:20,281 --> 00:34:22,300
to measure the distance between the Earth and the moon

646
00:34:22,301 --> 00:34:23,780
to within an inch.

647
00:34:23,781 --> 00:34:26,360
It still works today.

648
00:34:26,361 --> 00:34:30,900
After two hours and 31 minutes, the moonwalk came to an end.

649
00:34:30,901 --> 00:34:34,640
two men on another world grabbed their last few samples for the

650
00:34:34,641 --> 00:34:39,200
geologists back home. We were really not geologists.

651
00:34:39,201 --> 00:34:43,640
We were test pilots, fighter pilots in an unknown environment

652
00:34:43,641 --> 00:34:47,960
trying to do the best that we could, and it was up to a mix of

653
00:34:47,961 --> 00:34:52,460
later talents so that we would get the most scientific benefit

654
00:34:52,461 --> 00:34:56,860
out of the achievement of, as the president said, landing a man

655
00:34:56,861 --> 00:34:59,600
on the moon and bringing him back safely.

656
00:34:59,601 --> 00:35:02,900
Within the hour, they were back inside the LEM, preparing to

657
00:35:02,901 --> 00:35:06,000
depart the moon. Now the biggest cliffhanger of

658
00:35:06,001 --> 00:35:10,000
the mission loomed. To get home, the LEM ascent

659
00:35:10,001 --> 00:35:13,140
engine had to fire. It was another piece of technology

660
00:35:13,141 --> 00:35:15,660
that had never been tried on the moon.

661
00:35:15,661 --> 00:35:19,340
And to complicate matters, as they were leaving the cabin,

662
00:35:19,341 --> 00:35:25,040
they had broken the ascent engine switch.

663
00:35:25,041 --> 00:35:29,840
Lunar soil is comprised of approximately 30% to 60% glass,

664
00:35:29,841 --> 00:35:33,340
which is one reason why the moon reflects so much light and is

665
00:35:33,341 --> 00:35:37,780
brilliant in our night sky. Armstrong and Aldrin had been

666
00:35:37,781 --> 00:35:41,080
on the moon for a full 21 hours and had explored outside the

667
00:35:41,081 --> 00:35:46,400
LEM for 2 1⁄2 hours. Exhausted, they wanted to go

668
00:35:46,401 --> 00:35:49,740
home, but first they had to find a way to fire the ascent

669
00:35:49,741 --> 00:35:52,840
engine. The Grumman engineers huddled,

670
00:35:52,841 --> 00:35:56,840
pitching one idea after another to bypass the broken switch.

671
00:35:56,841 --> 00:36:00,840
Finally, a solution was found. The switch path was rerouted by

672
00:36:00,841 --> 00:36:03,840
Armstrong. Minutes later, he pressed the

673
00:36:03,841 --> 00:36:06,840
alternate ignition switch. The launch mechanism activated

674
00:36:06,841 --> 00:36:09,840
below them. Now, this sequence is

675
00:36:09,841 --> 00:36:12,840
completely automatic. Once they initiate the firing

676
00:36:12,841 --> 00:36:17,840
sequence, all the things that have to happen occur automatically.

677
00:36:17,841 --> 00:36:21,840
One of the big things that happens is that a guillotine

678
00:36:21,841 --> 00:36:25,240
the wires and all the connections that exist between the ascent

679
00:36:25,340 --> 00:36:28,240
and the descent stage. It also sends a command to the

680
00:36:28,340 --> 00:36:32,680
ascent engine to fire. Lunar liftoff was at

681
00:36:32,780 --> 00:36:36,680
124 hours, 22 minutes mission elapsed time.

682
00:36:36,780 --> 00:36:40,900
As the LEM ascended, it did so in an erratic weaving path.

683
00:36:41,000 --> 00:36:43,600
This was due to the fact that the two fuel tanks emptied at

684
00:36:43,700 --> 00:36:46,500
different rates, with fuels of differing densities.

685
00:36:46,600 --> 00:36:50,300
Since the center of gravity moves, in the ascent on the

686
00:36:50,301 --> 00:36:54,640
on the lunar module, in order to keep from firing thrusters

687
00:36:54,641 --> 00:36:59,400
kind of all the time, it wallows off of attitude,

688
00:36:59,401 --> 00:37:01,540
and then you thrust it back on.

689
00:37:01,541 --> 00:37:06,340
It does this a little bit in pitch and a little bit in roll.

690
00:37:06,341 --> 00:37:09,300
Then it'll do it more in roll and a little bit less in pitch.

691
00:37:09,301 --> 00:37:12,660
So it's really kind of a lazy maneuver,

692
00:37:12,661 --> 00:37:14,660
and you're along for the ride.

693
00:37:14,661 --> 00:37:17,100
As the two craft slowly approached each other,

694
00:37:17,101 --> 00:37:19,540
the command module piloted by Mike Collins

695
00:37:19,541 --> 00:37:23,700
took over the rendezvous. All Armstrong and Aldrin had to do

696
00:37:23,701 --> 00:37:26,820
was wait. Slowly, Collins closed on the

697
00:37:26,821 --> 00:37:30,440
docking target. As the two craft docked, very

698
00:37:30,441 --> 00:37:33,260
little of the Apollo rocket that departed Earth a week earlier was

699
00:37:33,261 --> 00:37:36,260
left. It was now 1 40th of its original

700
00:37:36,261 --> 00:37:38,780
mass. Once the docking latches took

701
00:37:38,781 --> 00:37:41,620
hold, Armstrong and Aldrin climbed back into the waiting

702
00:37:41,621 --> 00:37:45,180
command module. They were tired, but jubilant,

703
00:37:45,181 --> 00:37:48,180
as they prepared for the last rocket engine burn necessary to

704
00:37:48,181 --> 00:37:51,180
Once you get docked and the crew's transferred to the command

705
00:37:51,181 --> 00:37:54,820
module, that service propulsion system has to work.

706
00:37:54,821 --> 00:37:57,920
On the way home, Collins had a wry comment about the last

707
00:37:57,921 --> 00:38:01,120
firing of their rocket engine, which broke them free of lunar

708
00:38:01,121 --> 00:38:02,120
orbit.

709
00:38:02,121 --> 00:38:06,540
The SPS engine, our large rocket engine, on the aft end of our

710
00:38:06,541 --> 00:38:11,040
service module must have performed flawlessly or we would

711
00:38:11,041 --> 00:38:13,440
have been stranded in lunar orbit.

712
00:38:13,441 --> 00:38:16,980
As they sped back toward Earth, Armstrong decided to share a

713
00:38:16,981 --> 00:38:20,980
observations with Aldrin. One concerned the American flag

714
00:38:20,981 --> 00:38:24,980
they had planted. And he confidentially shared that

715
00:38:24,981 --> 00:38:27,980
it tipped over. And it doesn't look like it completely

716
00:38:27,981 --> 00:38:30,980
went over, but the exhaust was sure whipping the flag back and

717
00:38:30,981 --> 00:38:33,980
forth tremendously. About 60 hours later, it was

718
00:38:33,981 --> 00:38:35,980
time to let go of the service module.

719
00:38:35,981 --> 00:38:38,980
Its large rocket engine had taken them out of lunar orbit

720
00:38:38,981 --> 00:38:41,980
and prevented them from becoming, as one astronaut

721
00:38:41,981 --> 00:38:45,980
quipped, a permanent orbiting monument to NASA around the

722
00:38:45,981 --> 00:38:50,320
They were now approaching Earth at over 24,000 miles per hour.

723
00:38:50,321 --> 00:38:53,280
Any miscalculation would force them either to enter shallow

724
00:38:53,281 --> 00:38:56,620
and burn up or skip off the atmosphere.

725
00:38:56,621 --> 00:38:59,720
They knew their reentry alignment was critical.

726
00:38:59,721 --> 00:39:02,220
They aligned the command module to enter the atmosphere

727
00:39:02,221 --> 00:39:05,640
at a precise angle and began the fiery descent.

728
00:39:05,641 --> 00:39:09,640
With the air around the capsule ionized by the 5,000 degree heat,

729
00:39:09,641 --> 00:39:12,640
they would be out of contact with mission control.

730
00:39:12,641 --> 00:39:17,340
In the final stage of reentry, it's the crew and the spacecraft.

731
00:39:17,440 --> 00:39:18,740
We go through blackout.

732
00:39:18,840 --> 00:39:20,880
We've done the best we can for the crew.

733
00:39:20,980 --> 00:39:22,180
It's a difficult time.

734
00:39:22,280 --> 00:39:23,680
It's a lonely time for the controllers,

735
00:39:23,780 --> 00:39:25,980
because there's only one thought in every controller's mind.

736
00:39:26,080 --> 00:39:27,480
Did I give the crew everything we needed to?

737
00:39:27,580 --> 00:39:28,680
And was my day to write?

738
00:39:28,780 --> 00:39:30,800
There are several times in the mission

739
00:39:30,900 --> 00:39:32,000
where the crew's on their own.

740
00:39:32,100 --> 00:39:33,400
We've done the best we can.

741
00:39:33,500 --> 00:39:35,400
And then it's up to them and the spacecraft

742
00:39:35,500 --> 00:39:36,800
to finish off the mission.

743
00:39:36,900 --> 00:39:38,800
As the command module descended,

744
00:39:38,900 --> 00:39:41,340
the intense heat on the blunt end of the capsule

745
00:39:41,341 --> 00:39:44,640
the capsule began to melt and char the heat shield, as it was

746
00:39:44,641 --> 00:39:47,840
designed to do. The material, called ablative

747
00:39:47,841 --> 00:39:52,980
shielding, was designed to burn off and take the heat with it.

748
00:39:52,981 --> 00:39:56,320
As further protection against the 5,000 degree inferno, the

749
00:39:56,321 --> 00:39:59,320
shape of the capsule created a shock wave that also dissipated

750
00:39:59,321 --> 00:40:02,620
heat. As they left the fireball of

751
00:40:02,621 --> 00:40:05,940
reentry behind, explosive mortars on the capsule's top

752
00:40:05,941 --> 00:40:09,260
blasted small drogue parachutes, which would catch the air and

753
00:40:09,261 --> 00:40:12,300
and drag out the main chutes.

754
00:40:12,301 --> 00:40:13,800
These would break the landing speed

755
00:40:13,801 --> 00:40:15,800
to a leisurely 22 miles per hour.

756
00:40:19,500 --> 00:40:22,640
Soon, the astronauts were retrieved by Navy frogmen

757
00:40:22,641 --> 00:40:24,380
and transported to the carrier Hornet.

758
00:40:27,120 --> 00:40:31,480
But one more duty, longer than the mission itself remained.

759
00:40:31,481 --> 00:40:34,580
For the next 21 days, they were virtual shut-ins

760
00:40:34,581 --> 00:40:38,420
inside a specially designed germ-proof trailer.

761
00:40:38,421 --> 00:40:43,660
I think the quarantine in our case was not necessary.

762
00:40:43,661 --> 00:40:47,840
However, it was a welcome cushioning.

763
00:40:47,841 --> 00:40:50,720
It did give us time to reorder our priorities,

764
00:40:50,721 --> 00:40:54,140
to prepare for the things that we were going to have to do

765
00:40:54,141 --> 00:40:55,580
once we got out.

766
00:40:55,581 --> 00:40:58,040
Well, Neil had a funny statement about that,

767
00:40:58,041 --> 00:41:01,280
a good statement about that, which was, you know,

768
00:41:01,281 --> 00:41:03,440
the chances of them coming back to Earth

769
00:41:03,441 --> 00:41:08,440
with any kind of a moon bug were tiny, tiny, tiny, tiny.

770
00:41:09,020 --> 00:41:12,760
But the ramifications,

771
00:41:12,761 --> 00:41:16,760
if they did come back with a moon bug, were enormous.

772
00:41:16,761 --> 00:41:18,920
So you have a very, very, very small number

773
00:41:18,921 --> 00:41:21,200
times a very, very, very big number.

774
00:41:21,201 --> 00:41:25,320
And the result is, yeah, it's probably a good idea.

775
00:41:26,600 --> 00:41:28,200
Millions turned out to celebrate

776
00:41:28,201 --> 00:41:30,060
the returning space heroes.

777
00:41:30,061 --> 00:41:32,160
It was a high point of the 20th century.

778
00:41:33,440 --> 00:41:38,440
Back in Houston, the geologists cherished the rocks, all 47 pounds

779
00:41:38,441 --> 00:41:41,440
of them. The Apollo 11 moon rocks represented

780
00:41:41,441 --> 00:41:46,440
a surprising variety, considering the brief stay of their collectors.

781
00:41:46,441 --> 00:41:49,440
It was later said that Neil Armstrong had made one of the best

782
00:41:49,441 --> 00:41:53,440
sample collections of any astronaut on the moon.

783
00:41:53,441 --> 00:41:57,440
As the years pass and the memories of the first lunar landing begin

784
00:41:57,441 --> 00:42:01,440
to fade, we still have spectacular images to remember it by.

785
00:42:01,441 --> 00:42:05,180
some of the best were taken by the astronauts themselves.

786
00:42:05,181 --> 00:42:07,180
Well, I want a before and an after,

787
00:42:07,181 --> 00:42:11,080
so I took a picture of a flat surface,

788
00:42:11,081 --> 00:42:13,980
then I stuck my foot down and pulled it away,

789
00:42:13,981 --> 00:42:16,880
took a picture of that, and that looked so lonesome to me.

790
00:42:16,881 --> 00:42:20,100
I put my foot down another time and then moved my foot away

791
00:42:20,101 --> 00:42:22,900
slightly so you could see the boot.

792
00:42:22,901 --> 00:42:25,840
Six more Apollo missions would fly to the moon.

793
00:42:25,841 --> 00:42:28,600
With the near disaster of Apollo 13,

794
00:42:28,601 --> 00:42:33,100
only five would land. By the end of the program,

795
00:42:33,101 --> 00:42:36,600
the astronauts had a lunar rover and were capable of staying on

796
00:42:36,601 --> 00:42:40,300
the moon for three days. Hundreds of miles of the surface

797
00:42:40,301 --> 00:42:45,600
were explored, and over 842 pounds of rocks were returned.

798
00:42:45,601 --> 00:42:49,600
But by the time Apollo 17 flew, public support had all but

799
00:42:49,601 --> 00:42:52,600
vanished. Apollos 18, 19, and 20 were

800
00:42:52,601 --> 00:42:56,600
scrapped, and two of the Saturn launch vehicles languish in

801
00:42:56,601 --> 00:42:59,140
and museums today.

802
00:42:59,141 --> 00:43:02,700
Gene Cernan and Harrison Schmidt, the last men on the moon,

803
00:43:02,701 --> 00:43:06,440
left on December 12, 1972.

804
00:43:06,441 --> 00:43:09,020
Humanity has not returned.

805
00:43:09,021 --> 00:43:11,580
It's time to take the best minds that we have,

806
00:43:11,581 --> 00:43:15,080
not only within NASA, but within the technical community,

807
00:43:15,081 --> 00:43:18,080
come up with a proposal, cost this proposal,

808
00:43:18,081 --> 00:43:21,460
and then find some way to articulate it to the American

809
00:43:21,461 --> 00:43:24,360
people as necessary to survival.

810
00:43:24,361 --> 00:43:29,160
Our survival is our species and our survival economically,

811
00:43:29,161 --> 00:43:32,600
because the only way we're going to compete in this entire world

812
00:43:32,601 --> 00:43:34,040
is through technology.

813
00:43:34,041 --> 00:43:36,180
We need the nation behind us.

814
00:43:36,181 --> 00:43:38,480
We need the world behind us.

815
00:43:38,481 --> 00:43:41,680
And we need a unified appreciation

816
00:43:41,681 --> 00:43:43,840
that this is not going to break the bank,

817
00:43:43,841 --> 00:43:47,160
that everything we expend on exploration

818
00:43:47,161 --> 00:43:51,180
is going to be returned to us as a nation and us as a world

819
00:43:51,181 --> 00:43:52,880
many fold.

820
00:43:52,881 --> 00:43:56,080
The promise of America's space exploration

821
00:43:56,081 --> 00:44:00,360
is to move from magnificent desolation

822
00:44:00,361 --> 00:44:02,120
to magnificent inspiration.

